virglrenderer: Venus validates guest SPIR-V with SPIRV-Tools after the scan

What: a shader module (vkCreateShaderModule) and SPIR-V chained into a
pipeline stage go through spirv-val after vkr_spirv_scan, before the driver
sees them (src/venus/vkr_spirv_validate.c). The target follows the device, not
the app: its Vulkan version as vkr reports it (vulkan1.1spv1.4 on a 1.1 device
with VK_KHR_spirv_1_4) and the block layouts and modes its features allow
(scalar, standard uniform, scalar workgroup, LocalSizeId, relaxed), so code the
device runs passes. That version is the one the guest's driver tells the app:
KosmicKrisp reports its own, MoltenVK the instance's (at least 1.1, as vkr
creates it), so on MoltenVK an app that asks for 1.0 or 1.1 is told 1.1 and its
SPIR-V is validated as Vulkan 1.1's (up to SPIR-V 1.4). Before spirv-val reads
a module, it is refused unread when it is past 4 MiB, when its types cost more
than 2^24 (vkr_spirv.h,
VKR_SPIRV_MAX_TYPE_COST), or when an Input or Output variable of an entry
point takes more locations than the device's limits give its stage
(maxVertexInputAttributes, the component limits / 4,
maxFragmentOutputAttachments; VUID-RuntimeSpirv-Location-06272; a stage the
device lacks, as MoltenVK's geometry stage, keeps Vulkan's minimum); a pipeline
checks the locations again after specialization. spirv-val itself runs through
omacvmValidateWithOptions (spirv-tools-val-catch-throws.patch): a failed
assert, an exception or 2 s of CPU in it is a refusal, not the end of QEMU or a
stalled ring. Modules that passed are kept by the SHA-256 of target and code in
a bounded LRU cache (4096 entries, one mutex for all contexts; the validator
runs outside it); refusals are not kept. A refusal ends the app's context with
the first reason (VKR_SPIRV_VALIDATE_ENFORCE 0 at build time: spirv-val's
refusals are only logged, one line per module; the refusals before it stay).
A pipeline stage's Function and Private variables may hold at most 2^20
elements after specialization (arrays of arrays multiplied, struct members
added; a length from a spec constant operation is refused), and its Workgroup
variables at most maxComputeSharedMemorySize bytes (VUID-RuntimeSpirv-
Workgroup-06530; their sum without padding, the largest Block one when that
is more, a length from a spec constant operation counted once), both over the
variables the stage's code gets: the Function ones of the functions its entry
point reaches through calls (both Mac compilers translate only those), and the
Private and Workgroup ones of the module, or on KosmicKrisp from SPIR-V 1.4
only those in the entry point's interface (Mesa's spirv_to_nir skips the
others; MoltenVK's SPIRV-Cross declares every one in each entry point it
compiles, so there all of them count). vkCreateRayTracingPipelinesKHR
ends the context and VK_KHR_ray_tracing_pipeline is no longer offered: its
stages would reach the driver unchecked (neither Mac driver has it). qemu.log
names the target once per Vulkan version.
Why: of 900 modules fed to KosmicKrisp and MoltenVK on an M4 (compile only),
138 ended the process or hung the driver; the scan alone refused 72 of them,
spirv-val 65 of the other 66, the element cap the last one (an unused
uint[2^32-1], legal SPIR-V, took KosmicKrisp's nir_split_array_vars to 9 GB;
2^20 elements peaked at 480 MB in 0.5 s, 2^24 passed 2 GB in 2.4 s). A
Private or Workgroup uint[2^32-1] passed the scan and spirv-val too (Private
arrays were measured on the drivers only up to 2^24); real modules hold at
most 406 elements and 8192 Workgroup bytes (Dawn's matmul). The scan
stays first: it refuses two crash modules spirv-val accepts and costs 0.6 us a
module against spirv-val's 92 us (median of 126 real modules, 4.2 ms the
slowest; 52 us per KB). spirv-val on hostile input (CPU only, each run capped
at 512 MB inside the process): a 304-byte module with an Input struct of
uint[2^32-1] took it past 512 MB (location tracking per element; 7 of 7 fuzz
seeds found that class within seconds), a struct of two of the previous one 30
deep past 20 s, 1000 nested loops (88 KB) 53 s; with the checks above the
slowest of 118 such modules took 2.03 s (a chain of 262118 blocks, stopped at
2 s). valfuzz (one process, 10 minutes, capped at 512 MB inside it) found one
more crash in spirv-val on the final checks (seed 104: a BuiltIn untyped
variable, spirv-tools-val-catch-throws.patch); after its fix seed 104 again
(134.9 million mutants, 13.4 million through the scan) hit no cap and no
crash. Real modules cost at most 17002 and take at most 3.1 ms. With the final checks on the CPU, the 90 real modules of the CPU
test's corpus (and their 16 paired vertex shaders) and vkd3d-proton 3.1's 42
pass for both drivers' targets; under a 1.0 or 1.1 app on MoltenVK the 37
past SPIR-V 1.4 are refused. Earlier, spirv-val alone on 126 real modules:
vulkan1.3 defaults refused 5 (DXVK's d3d9 and vkd3d-proton use scalar
layouts), a vulkan1.2 target 71 SPIR-V 1.6 modules.
Upstream: OmacVM policy, not for upstream as is (it needs SPIRV-Tools).
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-venus-spirv-validate.sh (the CPU alone, also under
AddressSanitizer, each module under a 512 MB footprint cap, for KosmicKrisp,
MoltenVK and MoltenVK under a 1.1 app: real modules pass, the crash and
hostile modules are refused, the cache, the targets, the Function and Private
element count, the Workgroup bytes per entry point (two compute entry points
whose Function variables are over the cap together pass), the interface
locations per stage from the drivers' own limits, the type cost),
Tests/virgl/fuzz-venus-spirv.sh (the element count, the Workgroup bytes and
the interface check under the sanitizers). Hostile modules never reach a real driver in a test
(STANDARDS 14b).

